WEBVTT

00:00:00.304 --> 00:00:05.734
Take two crystals that block
electric current, LaAlO₃ and SrTiO₃.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.820
Grow a few layers of one on the other,
and the boundary between them conducts.

00:00:10.241 --> 00:00:12.251
Fifty research papers tell this story.

00:00:12.437 --> 00:00:14.627
They cover electrons
trapped at the boundary,

00:00:15.057 --> 00:00:16.827
heat carried by atomic vibrations,

00:00:17.197 --> 00:00:19.307
and a computer method that predicts both.

00:00:20.471 --> 00:00:24.641
We will build up to it in twelve steps,
starting from IGCSE physics.

00:00:25.022 --> 00:00:26.872
Each step uses the ones before it.

00:00:27.671 --> 00:00:31.921
Some things shrink by the same fraction
each step, like radioactive decay.

00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:35.167
We write this with the number e,
about 2.718.

00:00:35.628 --> 00:00:39.578
The function e to the x changes
at a rate equal to its own value.

00:00:39.774 --> 00:00:42.704
The same shape returns later
as the Boltzmann factor.

00:00:43.671 --> 00:00:45.041
A wave needs two rates.

00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:48.302
Angular frequency, omega,
counts phase per second.

00:00:48.498 --> 00:00:50.958
Wavenumber, k, counts phase per metre.

00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:54.519
Dividing one by the other gives
the wave speed, f times lambda.

00:00:54.835 --> 00:00:58.485
Euler's formula turns a wave into
a point turning round a circle.

00:00:59.338 --> 00:01:01.588
A matrix turns one vector into another.

00:01:01.774 --> 00:01:05.164
This one keeps the vector one,
one the same, and stretches one,

00:01:05.174 --> 00:01:06.504
minus one by three.

00:01:06.685 --> 00:01:11.155
These special vectors are eigenvectors,
and the stretch factors are eigenvalues.

00:01:11.531 --> 00:01:16.081
In physics, eigenvalues give vibration
frequencies and allowed energies.

00:01:17.004 --> 00:01:19.839
A spring pulls back with
a force of minus k x.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:24.780
Newton's second law then gives a
vibration at omega equals root k over m.

00:01:24.941 --> 00:01:27.201
Bonds between atoms behave like springs.

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:31.172
A typical atom on its bond
vibrates at about five terahertz.

00:01:31.938 --> 00:01:34.828
Now line up a long chain
of atoms joined by springs.

00:01:35.249 --> 00:01:37.759
A wave along it obeys
a dispersion relation,

00:01:38.089 --> 00:01:40.039
which links frequency to wavenumber.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.050
Long waves are ordinary sound.

00:01:42.276 --> 00:01:45.646
The frequency peaks when neighbours
move in opposite directions.

00:01:45.912 --> 00:01:50.862
Every distinct wave fits between minus pi
over a and pi over a, the Brillouin zone.

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:54.661
Send a wave into a heavier rope,
and part of it reflects.

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:58.277
The reflected share depends
on the impedance of each side.

00:01:58.478 --> 00:02:02.528
If one impedance is twice the other,
89 percent of the energy passes.

00:02:02.844 --> 00:02:04.854
So every interface reflects some heat.

00:02:05.065 --> 00:02:09.285
A stack of repeating layers reflects
waves with a wavelength of twice the layer

00:02:09.285 --> 00:02:09.834
spacing.

00:02:10.871 --> 00:02:14.981
A huge flat sheet of charge makes a
field that does not weaken with distance.

00:02:15.357 --> 00:02:17.707
Put a positive sheet
next to a negative one,

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.527
and the field is trapped between them.

00:02:19.653 --> 00:02:22.123
A steady field across a
distance builds a voltage,

00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:23.893
V equals E d.

00:02:23.904 --> 00:02:26.534
This one line will explain
the oxide interface.

00:02:28.058 --> 00:02:30.328
A capacitor stores charge
when a voltage is applied.

00:02:30.584 --> 00:02:33.174
If one plate is a sheet of
electrons in a material,

00:02:33.504 --> 00:02:35.874
the voltage adds or removes electrons.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.230
The metal plate is then called a gate.

00:02:38.496 --> 00:02:42.966
Cold SrTiO₃ has a dielectric
constant near twenty thousand,

00:02:43.016 --> 00:02:45.906
so a back gate can switch
an interface on and off.

00:02:46.771 --> 00:02:50.181
In a field, electrons drift at
a speed set by their mobility.

00:02:50.587 --> 00:02:55.277
A magnetic field pushes moving electrons
to one edge, which makes the Hall voltage.

00:02:55.568 --> 00:02:57.238
That voltage counts the carriers,

00:02:57.568 --> 00:03:00.198
and nearly every experiment
in the papers uses it.

00:03:00.904 --> 00:03:04.934
Heat flows from hot to cold at a
rate set by the thermal conductivity.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.795
In insulators, vibrations carry the heat,

00:03:08.025 --> 00:03:10.355
so the mean free path
between collisions matters.

00:03:10.666 --> 00:03:13.896
In silicon that path is
about 40 nanometres or more.

00:03:14.092 --> 00:03:16.402
Devices smaller than
this break Fourier's law.

00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:20.348
Light arrives in packets,
each with energy h f.

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:23.789
Particles behave as waves,
with a wavelength of h over p.

00:03:24.005 --> 00:03:28.718
An electron with one electron volt of
energy has a wavelength of about 1.2

00:03:28.718 --> 00:03:29.755
nanometres.

00:03:30.081 --> 00:03:33.851
The Schrödinger equation for these
waves allows only special energies.

00:03:34.838 --> 00:03:38.728
Trap a particle between two walls,
and only whole half wavelengths fit.

00:03:38.939 --> 00:03:40.849
The energy levels grow as n squared.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.850
In a five nanometre layer
of gallium arsenide,

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:49.590
the first gap is 0.67 electron volts,
far above room temperature heat.

00:03:49.786 --> 00:03:53.656
So motion across the layer freezes,
while motion along it stays free.

00:03:53.852 --> 00:03:56.722
That flat sheet is a two
dimensional electron gas.

00:03:57.638 --> 00:04:01.968
Real walls have a finite height, so the
wavefunction leaks a little way into them.

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:06.089
At an interface, an electric field
presses electrons against a wall,

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:07.709
making a triangular well.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.370
A stronger field squeezes the
electrons closer to the interface.

00:04:12.338 --> 00:04:17.128
A quantum spring has evenly spaced
energy levels, one ħω apart.

00:04:17.294 --> 00:04:21.444
In a crystal, one lump of vibration
energy in one mode is a phonon.

00:04:21.675 --> 00:04:25.505
Even the lowest level keeps some energy,
so atoms never stop moving.

00:04:26.404 --> 00:04:27.874
In SrTiO₃,

00:04:27.984 --> 00:04:32.414
titanium gives away four electrons
and keeps empty 3d orbitals.

00:04:32.555 --> 00:04:35.825
Extra electrons at an interface
go into those orbitals.

00:04:35.966 --> 00:04:38.196
Each orbital holds two electrons at most,

00:04:38.566 --> 00:04:40.456
one spin up and one spin down.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.307
Many electrons together are hard,

00:04:42.307 --> 00:04:46.337
because the wavefunction needs three
coordinates for every electron.

00:04:47.171 --> 00:04:50.121
Temperature sets a typical
thermal energy, kT.

00:04:50.362 --> 00:04:54.392
At room temperature it
is 0.026 electron volts.

00:04:54.688 --> 00:04:58.578
A state higher by delta E is less
likely by the Boltzmann factor.

00:04:58.924 --> 00:05:00.694
Cooling shuts things off exponentially,

00:05:01.284 --> 00:05:03.894
so many effects appear
only near absolute zero.

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:08.994
In a flat sheet, the allowed electron
waves form a grid of dots in k space.

00:05:09.355 --> 00:05:11.585
Counting the dots gives
the density of states,

00:05:11.895 --> 00:05:13.625
which is constant in two dimensions.

00:05:13.996 --> 00:05:17.846
Electrons fill the states up to the
Fermi energy, and a gate tunes it.

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:21.767
Only electrons near the Fermi
energy can conduct or take up heat.

00:05:22.638 --> 00:05:26.268
When cold, only phonons with
energy below kT are awake.

00:05:26.539 --> 00:05:30.569
Counting them gives a heat capacity
that grows as T cubed in 3D,

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:33.189
T squared in 2D and T in 1D.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.510
A membrane 100 nanometres thick acts
2D for phonons below about one kelvin.

00:05:39.564 --> 00:05:45.534
SrTiO₃ has the perovskite structure,
a cube 0.39 nanometres wide.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:50.440
Strontium sits at the corners, titanium
at the centre, and oxygen on the faces.

00:05:50.786 --> 00:05:51.896
Seen from the side,

00:05:52.066 --> 00:05:56.916
it is a stack of alternating
SrO and TiO₂ layers.

00:05:57.207 --> 00:06:00.697
LaAlO₃ grows on top,
stretched by about three percent.

00:06:01.938 --> 00:06:05.738
An electron wave with a wavelength
of twice the atom spacing reflects

00:06:05.738 --> 00:06:06.608
completely.

00:06:06.929 --> 00:06:10.799
Its two standing waves have different
energies, so an energy gap opens.

00:06:11.085 --> 00:06:15.175
From the atom side, hopping between
neighbours spreads each level into a band.

00:06:15.431 --> 00:06:17.201
A steep band means light electrons,

00:06:17.651 --> 00:06:19.301
and a flat band means heavy ones.

00:06:20.204 --> 00:06:22.054
A partly filled band makes a metal.

00:06:22.245 --> 00:06:25.795
Full and empty bands with a big
gap between them make an insulator.

00:06:26.151 --> 00:06:29.841
SrTiO₃ has a gap of 3.2 electron volts,

00:06:30.151 --> 00:06:32.961
and LaAlO₃ about 5.6.

00:06:33.904 --> 00:06:35.454
Inside its oxygen cage,

00:06:36.044 --> 00:06:38.334
titanium's d orbitals
split into two groups.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:41.180
The three that point between
the oxygens sit lowest.

00:06:41.456 --> 00:06:45.726
The dxy orbital lies flat, light
within the plane and heavy across it.

00:06:45.857 --> 00:06:49.187
So dxy electrons sit lowest
and hug the interface.

00:06:50.171 --> 00:06:53.561
A missing oxygen ion leaves
up to two electrons behind,

00:06:53.831 --> 00:06:56.081
so oxygen vacancies dope the crystal.

00:06:56.362 --> 00:07:00.052
Where two materials meet, charge
flows until their Fermi levels match.

00:07:00.363 --> 00:07:04.133
The bands bend, and electrons collect
in a narrow well at the interface.

00:07:05.104 --> 00:07:08.854
With two kinds of atom in each cell,
vibrations split into two branches.

00:07:09.275 --> 00:07:12.625
In the acoustic branch,
neighbours move together, as in sound.

00:07:12.861 --> 00:07:16.431
In the optical branch they move
against each other, at high frequency.

00:07:16.797 --> 00:07:19.067
Between the branches lies a phonon gap.

00:07:19.971 --> 00:07:23.521
Real bonds are slightly anharmonic,
so phonons can collide.

00:07:23.787 --> 00:07:26.217
Normal collisions keep
heat flowing forward.

00:07:26.423 --> 00:07:29.893
Umklapp collisions turn it backward
and create thermal resistance.

00:07:30.359 --> 00:07:33.629
So a pure crystal's conductivity
rises from the cold, peaks,

00:07:33.839 --> 00:07:35.569
then falls as one over T.

00:07:36.438 --> 00:07:41.008
In SrTiO₃, some vibrations get
softer as the crystal cools.

00:07:41.094 --> 00:07:45.784
A rotation of the oxygen cages
freezes in at about 105 kelvin.

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.440
A polar mode never freezes,
because of zero point motion.

00:07:49.641 --> 00:07:54.891
As it softens, the dielectric constant
climbs from 300 to above ten thousand.

00:07:55.704 --> 00:08:00.334
In a ferroelectric, the titanium ion
sits slightly off centre, up or down.

00:08:00.505 --> 00:08:04.015
Every cell gets a dipole,
and a strong field can flip them all.

00:08:04.156 --> 00:08:07.786
The surface charge is about
10¹⁴ per square centimetre,

00:08:07.996 --> 00:08:10.486
as large as an oxide electron gas.

00:08:10.772 --> 00:08:11.782
Flip the polarization,

00:08:12.272 --> 00:08:15.362
and an electron gas at the
interface switches on or off.

00:08:16.138 --> 00:08:20.248
In a ferromagnet, electron spins line
up because of the exchange interaction.

00:08:20.749 --> 00:08:23.599
The spin up and spin down
bands shift apart in energy.

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.080
A current from such a
metal is spin polarized,

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:27.920
the basis of spintronics.

00:08:28.904 --> 00:08:32.274
Below a critical temperature,
some materials lose all resistance.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:36.280
Electrons attract each other through
the lattice and form Cooper pairs.

00:08:36.611 --> 00:08:40.011
The oxide interface superconducts
below about 0.2 kelvin,

00:08:40.011 --> 00:08:42.521
in a sheet ten nanometres thick.

00:08:42.747 --> 00:08:45.917
A back gate tunes it between
superconductor and insulator.

00:08:46.838 --> 00:08:49.528
When electrons crowd
into narrow d orbitals,

00:08:49.658 --> 00:08:51.628
their repulsion U competes with hopping.

00:08:51.909 --> 00:08:55.259
If U beats the band width,
electrons freeze one per atom,

00:08:55.449 --> 00:08:56.839
which makes a Mott insulator.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:01.070
LaTiO₃ is one,
and band theory alone cannot predict it.

00:09:01.871 --> 00:09:05.601
A sheet is fully two dimensional when
only the first subband is filled.

00:09:05.867 --> 00:09:09.717
The oxide interface fills more than one,
so it is quasi two dimensional.

00:09:10.018 --> 00:09:12.748
Electron gases can come from doping,
polarization,

00:09:13.338 --> 00:09:15.988
a gate, a polar step or one doped layer.

00:09:17.504 --> 00:09:20.254
Scattering rates add,
so the lowest mobility wins.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.450
Clean gallium arsenide
reaches over ten million.

00:09:23.756 --> 00:09:26.496
The oxide interface reaches
up to ten thousand when cold,

00:09:26.496 --> 00:09:28.806
and only a few at room temperature.

00:09:29.771 --> 00:09:32.521
A magnetic field bends
electrons into circles.

00:09:32.772 --> 00:09:36.982
Quantum mechanics turns their energies
into evenly spaced Landau levels.

00:09:37.213 --> 00:09:38.123
In a clean sheet,

00:09:38.233 --> 00:09:43.783
the Hall resistance locks to 25,813
ohms divided by a whole number.

00:09:43.979 --> 00:09:46.849
These plateaus are precise
enough to define the ohm.

00:09:47.504 --> 00:09:51.354
In a narrow wire without scattering,
the electron speed cancels out.

00:09:51.525 --> 00:09:56.815
Each channel conducts two e squared
over h, about one over 12.9 kilohms.

00:09:56.931 --> 00:10:01.541
At the oxide interface, a microscope
tip can draw such wires and erase them.

00:10:01.672 --> 00:10:04.882
One sketched transistor
switched at over one gigahertz.

00:10:06.484 --> 00:10:11.374
In 2004, Ohtomo and Hwang
grew LaAlO₃ on SrTiO₃.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.000
The boundary between the
two insulators conducted.

00:10:14.406 --> 00:10:16.336
Most of the reference list asks why.

00:10:17.204 --> 00:10:19.374
Count the charge in each atomic layer.

00:10:19.575 --> 00:10:22.625
In SrTiO₃, both kinds
of layer are neutral.

00:10:22.946 --> 00:10:27.616
In LaAlO₃, the layers alternate
between plus one and minus one.

00:10:27.772 --> 00:10:31.442
Starting on titanium dioxide
gives a conducting interface,

00:10:31.792 --> 00:10:35.422
and one extra layer of strontium
oxide makes it insulate.

00:10:36.238 --> 00:10:39.388
Those charged layers act like
the sheets from chapter three.

00:10:39.524 --> 00:10:41.494
The field is on in half of every cell,

00:10:41.724 --> 00:10:44.474
so the voltage climbs by
about one volt per cell.

00:10:44.635 --> 00:10:49.345
Five cells would build almost five volts,
and that runaway is the polar catastrophe.

00:10:50.471 --> 00:10:55.341
After about 3.3 cells,
the build up passes the gap of SrTiO₃.

00:10:55.517 --> 00:10:59.467
Half an electron per cell then moves
from the surface down to the interface.

00:10:59.733 --> 00:11:01.143
The voltage stops climbing.

00:11:01.434 --> 00:11:06.164
This predicts 3.3 times 10¹⁴
electrons per square centimetre.

00:11:07.004 --> 00:11:08.514
Grown one cell at a time,

00:11:08.584 --> 00:11:11.894
the interface insulates up to
three cells and conducts at four.

00:11:12.105 --> 00:11:15.835
Diluting the layer charge raised that
critical thickness, as predicted.

00:11:16.191 --> 00:11:19.941
Hall measurements find about thirty
times fewer electrons than predicted,

00:11:20.291 --> 00:11:22.261
and where the rest go is still debated.

00:11:23.204 --> 00:11:26.234
Oxygen vacancies are a
rival source of electrons.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:30.070
Samples grown in low oxygen
conduct deep into the substrate.

00:11:30.296 --> 00:11:31.686
After annealing in oxygen,

00:11:32.036 --> 00:11:34.866
conduction shrinks to a few
nanometres at the interface.

00:11:35.102 --> 00:11:37.232
So always check how a sample was grown.

00:11:38.171 --> 00:11:40.641
The electron gas is a
few nanometres thick.

00:11:40.862 --> 00:11:45.612
It superconducts, shows magnetic effects,
and makes rewritable nanoscale devices.

00:11:45.938 --> 00:11:49.568
A fast transistor in such a thin
sheet must also shed its heat,

00:11:49.658 --> 00:11:51.328
and phonons carry it away.

00:11:52.171 --> 00:11:56.181
In a large sample, phonons collide
many times, and heat diffuses.

00:11:56.427 --> 00:11:59.857
In a sample shorter than the mean
free path, they fly straight across.

00:12:00.143 --> 00:12:03.493
Below about one kelvin,
mean free paths reach micrometres.

00:12:04.404 --> 00:12:07.434
Join a hot and a cold
reservoir with a narrow wire.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.610
As with electrons,
the phonon speed cancels out.

00:12:10.791 --> 00:12:15.161
Every perfect channel carries the same
heat, the quantum of thermal conductance.

00:12:15.317 --> 00:12:18.407
At one kelvin it is about
one picowatt per kelvin.

00:12:19.338 --> 00:12:22.908
Theory predicted four channels per
wire at the lowest temperatures.

00:12:23.339 --> 00:12:27.859
Schwab and colleagues hung a tiny island
from four bridges and cooled it below one

00:12:27.859 --> 00:12:28.569
kelvin.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:31.130
Its conductance levelled
off at sixteen quanta,

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:33.370
four bridges times four channels.

00:12:34.304 --> 00:12:36.574
A thermal diode lets heat flow one way.

00:12:36.745 --> 00:12:39.895
Two joined chains pass heat
only at frequencies they share,

00:12:40.185 --> 00:12:41.935
and temperature shifts those frequencies.

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:45.281
A real one, made from
unevenly loaded nanotubes,

00:12:45.281 --> 00:12:47.851
passed heat a few percent better one way.

00:12:48.671 --> 00:12:53.641
A superlattice stacks thin layers of
gallium arsenide and aluminium arsenide.

00:12:53.707 --> 00:12:55.737
If each interface scattered phonons,

00:12:56.287 --> 00:12:58.577
conductivity would stay
flat as the stack grows.

00:12:58.853 --> 00:13:01.283
Measured conductivity
rose with total thickness,

00:13:01.473 --> 00:13:03.923
so the phonons crossed as coherent waves.

00:13:04.904 --> 00:13:08.814
Every interface causes a temperature jump,
the thermal boundary resistance.

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:13.800
A typical one resists heat as
much as 150 nanometres of silicon.

00:13:14.126 --> 00:13:17.716
Simple models treat the boundary
as a smooth or a rough mismatch.

00:13:18.804 --> 00:13:22.894
The atomistic Green's function method
starts from every atom's mass and springs.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:26.580
It asks how the atoms respond
when shaken at each frequency.

00:13:26.931 --> 00:13:30.561
From that it reads off the transmission,
and the heat conductance follows.

00:13:31.504 --> 00:13:34.454
Storing the full wavefunction
of a crystal is impossible.

00:13:34.895 --> 00:13:37.925
Density functional theory works
with the electron density,

00:13:37.995 --> 00:13:40.045
one function of three coordinates.

00:13:41.381 --> 00:13:45.171
In 1964, Hohenberg and Kohn proved
that the density fixes everything.

00:13:46.071 --> 00:13:50.641
Kohn and Sham imagined non interacting
electrons with the same density.

00:13:50.822 --> 00:13:55.292
The energy splits into four parts, and
only exchange and correlation are unknown.

00:13:55.658 --> 00:14:00.628
Approximations called LDA,
GGA and DFT+U fill that gap.

00:14:01.438 --> 00:14:03.348
The calculation runs in a loop.

00:14:03.549 --> 00:14:07.139
Guess a density, build the potential,
and solve for the orbitals.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:09.900
Fill them to get a new density,
and go round again.

00:14:10.096 --> 00:14:13.086
When nothing changes,
the result is self consistent.

00:14:14.038 --> 00:14:17.308
Orbitals are built from plane waves,
up to a cutoff energy.

00:14:17.554 --> 00:14:21.204
Pseudopotentials and PAW
smooth out the core electrons.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:24.720
Averages over the Brillouin
zone use a grid of k points,

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:26.760
such as eight by eight by one.

00:14:26.921 --> 00:14:29.591
An interface is modelled
by a tall supercell,

00:14:29.721 --> 00:14:34.551
such as one LaAlO₃ cell
on 23 SrTiO₃ cells.

00:14:35.471 --> 00:14:39.421
A layer projected density of states
shows where the electron gas sits.

00:14:39.682 --> 00:14:44.012
Its weight is strongest in the first
titanium layer and fades in deeper ones.

00:14:44.218 --> 00:14:46.888
Standard DFT underestimates gaps,

00:14:47.138 --> 00:14:52.068
giving about 2 electron volts
for SrTiO₃ against 3.2 measured.

00:14:52.971 --> 00:14:56.921
Nudge each atom and recompute the forces,
and you get the spring constants.

00:14:57.322 --> 00:14:59.912
Their eigenvalues give
the phonon dispersion.

00:15:00.193 --> 00:15:02.443
Those springs feed the
Green's function method.

00:15:02.654 --> 00:15:06.554
Together they predict both the electron
gas and the heat flow at an oxide

00:15:06.554 --> 00:15:07.464
interface.

00:15:08.371 --> 00:15:12.721
Each step built on the one before, from
springs and waves to bands and phonons.

00:15:12.942 --> 00:15:18.302
Charged layers in LaAlO₃ push electrons
into a thin sheet at the boundary with

00:15:18.302 --> 00:15:19.732
SrTiO₃.

00:15:19.893 --> 00:15:21.563
Phonons carry its heat away,

00:15:21.733 --> 00:15:25.103
and density functional theory
predicts both from the atoms up.

